  "body": "Part of #308. Found while researching it (code reading on `main` 2026-11-08; **not yet reproduced by a test - the first step is a pinning test**).\n\n## What happens\nA work offset can carry a rotation: `SetWcsOrigin` stores `rot`, and the 3-2-1 alignment (#187, PR #201) sets roll/pitch/yaw from a picked face and edge. Posting passes only the offset's **translation**:\n- `posting::wcs_context` returns `(slot, origin)` with `origin = [pos.x, pos.y, pos.z]` - the rotation is dropped;\n- the post engine emits only `OriginX/Y/Z` (`crates/post/src/post/mod.rs`), and the shipped posts (e.g. `fanuc.cps` `wo()`) subtract that origin from world coordinates.\n\nSo a toolpath for a part aligned with a rotated work offset posts world-axis coordinates shifted by the origin, not rotated into the offset's frame - on the machine, a toolpath turned against the part, with no diagnostic. No test posts with a non-identity offset rotation (every posting/setups test uses `IDENTITY_Q`).\n\n## Fix\n1. Pinning test: place G54 with a 90 deg yaw (or via `align_321` on a turned block), generate a contour, post; assert what the program says.\n2. Minimum safe fix (Prime Directive): the post gate - and generation's law checks, which read `wcs_context` too - refuse by name an operation whose work offset carries a non-identity rotation, e.g. \"work offset G54 is rotated (3-2-1 alignment); posting a rotated offset is not supported yet\".\n3. Then real support, as a user parameter with a default: the post rotates program coordinates into the offset frame, or emits the control's rotation (Fanuc G68 / G68.2, Siemens CYCLE800 / ROT, Heidenhain PLANE / CYCL 10) where the machine declares it. A G54 register is translation only on every control; rotation is either baked into the coordinates by the post or done by a control cycle. Public control documentation only.\n\n## Done means\nA rotated work offset either posts correctly rotated coordinates (pinned against a hand-computed program) or is refused by name - never a silent unrotated program.\n",
